- Weight or contribution.
- Mandatory or optional status.
- Ordering.
- Whether it contributes to the final subject result.
- Conversion method.
- Applicable class.
- Applicable academic year.
- Applicable examination.
- Applicable subject.

### Example configuration A

Bangla:

- Written: 70.
- MCQ: 30.
- Total: 100.

### Example configuration B

Mathematics:

- Written: 50.
- MCQ: 25.
- Practical: 25.
- Total: 100.

### Example configuration C

English:

- Paper 1: 50.
- Paper 2: 50.
- Combined subject total: 100.

These are examples only. Administrators must be able to create other configurations.

### Validation rules

- Validate that the configured distribution matches the intended subject full marks.
- If weights are used, validate the weights according to the selected calculation policy.
- Show errors when totals or weights are inconsistent.
- Do not silently change any configured value.
- Do not allow entered marks to exceed component maximum marks.
- Allow different subjects to have different distributions.
- Allow different examinations to have different distributions.
- Allow distributions to be copied from another examination or academic year, with administrator confirmation.
- Allow administrators to preview the resulting calculation.

Maintain a version history whenever a configuration changes.

